WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.020
Sema3A is made in the ventral cord.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.660
Pain fibres stay out, and muscle afferents go in.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.620
Is the difference in the cue, or in the axons?

4
00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:19.353
Here is the test. Set a dorsal root ganglion in a collagen gel, beside a piece of

5
00:00:19.353 --> 00:00:20.353
ventral cord.

6
00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:25.023
Grow it once with nerve growth factor and once with neurotrophin-3 (NT-3).

7
00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:28.463
The medium largely decides which neurons grow.

8
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:31.523
Where will the axons grow in each dish?

9
00:00:37.517 --> 00:00:40.277
With NGF, the pain fibres grow.

10
00:00:41.137 --> 00:00:43.297
They grow poorly on the side facing the cord.

11
00:00:44.137 --> 00:00:46.477
The ring of axons, the halo, is lopsided.

12
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.340
With NT-3, the muscle afferents grow.

13
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.580
Their halo is nearly round.

14
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.320
Same tissue, two answers.

15
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.240
Messersmith, Kolodkin and colleagues found this pattern in rat explants.

16
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.080
Cells secreting Sema3A did the same.

17
00:01:08.283 --> 00:01:10.583
So how does a growth cone hear Sema3A?

18
00:01:11.383 --> 00:01:13.343
Two groups found the receptor independently.

19
00:01:14.403 --> 00:01:15.403
It was neuropilin.

20
00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:18.743
Neuropilin binds Sema3A tightly.

21
00:01:19.583 --> 00:01:23.183
But its tail inside the cell is short, with nothing like an enzyme.

22
00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:25.183
Where is the wire?

23
00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:27.973
Two more groups pointed to the plexins.

24
00:01:28.893 --> 00:01:31.973
These membrane proteins have a large part inside the cell.

25
00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:38.093
In engineered cells, neuropilin alone binds the cue, but the cell does not respond.

26
00:01:38.993 --> 00:01:41.033
With plexin as well, binding is tighter.

27
00:01:42.073 --> 00:01:43.493
Now the cells shrink and round up.

28
00:01:45.783 --> 00:01:49.643
Other work tested neurons, which keep their other receptor parts.

29
00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:54.383
There, a neuropilin with no inner tail still let growth cones collapse.

30
00:01:57.167 --> 00:02:01.407
Now give sensory neurons extra plexin with its inner part cut off.

31
00:02:02.247 --> 00:02:04.187
They still have their own normal plexin.

32
00:02:05.007 --> 00:02:06.007
What happens?

33
00:02:11.617 --> 00:02:12.977
The response is blocked.

34
00:02:13.857 --> 00:02:17.437
The broken plexin interferes with the cell's own working receptors.

35
00:02:18.397 --> 00:02:23.437
One likely idea: it joins neuropilin and the cue in complexes that cannot signal.

36
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.140
A mutant that blocks the normal protein this way is called a dominant negative.

37
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.360
So neuropilin catches, and plexin signals.

38
00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:39.613
Inside, the plexin switches off a small GTPase of the Ras family.

39
00:02:40.573 --> 00:02:42.773
That protein helps integrins grip the surface.

40
00:02:43.713 --> 00:02:46.953
Other plexin pathways act on actin and microtubules.

41
00:02:47.873 --> 00:02:50.333
Together, the growth cone loses grip and actin.

42
00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:57.187
In a growth cone that Sema3A repels, where this happens shapes what you see.

43
00:02:58.387 --> 00:03:03.147
Typically, a gradient from one side turns it away, and a strong dose all round can

44
00:03:03.147 --> 00:03:06.267
fold the fan up, in preparations that support collapse.

45
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:13.130
The takeaway: for Sema3A, the working receptor is neuropilin and plexin together.

46
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.050
No pair, no answer in these experiments.
